<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Declares only what CMSNApp code actually does.

	  Not collected (stays on device, Apple's "collect" = sent off device
	  and retained past the request): SwiftData profile, workouts, runs,
	  routes, shoes, nutrition, supplements, and score; EventKit titles
	  read locally to match a scheduled workout; VisionKit barcode frames
	  (no photo is written); a photo the athlete attaches to a run;
	  location and heart rate used only during that run; StoreKit
	  entitlement state read on device. Purchases are processed by Apple.
	  Apple Health receives a finished run only after the athlete allows
	  it. Nothing in the run path is an analytics SDK.

	  Open Food Facts receives a barcode or search string to answer that
	  lookup. The request does not include a name, account, or device
	  identifier, and CMSN does not retain it on a server. Data used only
	  to service the request is not collected.

	  NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes stays empty on purpose: under Apple's
	  definition nothing above is collected. Re-audit before adding any
	  analytics, crash reporter, account, or sync backend.

	  NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes re-audited 2026-11-05. CMSNApp does not call
	  file-timestamp APIs (stat, getattrlist, NSFileCreationDate,
	  NSURLContentModificationDateKey, and the related keys), system boot
	  time, disk space, or active keyboards. Those categories are not
	  declared. There is no third-party SDK. Run preferences live on
	  RunSettings, not UserDefaults. CA92.1 stays from the previous
	  manifest: it is Apple's reason for this app's own defaults only
	  (no app group). It is not a new reason. System frameworks ship
	  their own manifests.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
